course: dictionary
lesson: 10992
title: Warthog
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A warthog is a wild pig that lives on the African grasslands. Its name gives you a clue: it has lumps on its face that look like warts. Read the words around a word to find its meaning.
explain: «A warthog's warts are thick bumpy pads that protect its face in fights.» Warts here does not mean the skin problem. The sentence says the bumps are thick pads that protect the face, so the warts are the bumpy lumps.
explain: «Warthogs often use old aardvark burrows and back in tail-first, so their tusks face the opening.» A burrow is a hole dug in the ground for shelter. Backing in tail-first means the tusks point out at the way in, ready to defend.
fact: A warthog is a noun: a wild pig of the African grasslands.
fact: «Warthogs» are wild pigs that mostly eat grass, roots and bulbs.
fact: A warthog has a big flat head, long curved tusks and a thin mane.
fact: «A running warthog holds its thin tail straight up» and can run at about 50 km per hour.
fact: A group of warthogs is called «a sounder of warthogs».
exercise: What is a warthog? | A small bird that lives in the desert | A large cat that hunts at night | *A wild pig of the African grasslands | A kind of grass that grows in the savannah
exercise: «Warthogs are wild pigs that mostly eat grass, roots and bulbs.» What do warthogs mostly eat? | *Grass, roots and bulbs | Meat from lions | Berries from trees | Fish and seals
exercise: Find and copy the word for a group of warthogs in «a sounder of warthogs». | tusk | mane | *sounder | burrow
test: «A running warthog holds its thin tail straight up.» Why might it do this? | *It may help its young follow it through tall grass | It is sneezing | It is swimming in a river | It is hiding from the sun
test: Why does a warthog back into a burrow? | So it can dig deeper for roots | So its tail can point at the sun | So it can sleep faster | *So its tusks face the opening, ready to defend itself
exam: Pumbaa in «The Lion King» is which animal? | Lion | Aardvark | Meerkat | *Warthog
